speechtext:
Mr. Chairman, before the six o'clock recess, the hon. member for Bona-vista-Twillingate (Mr. Pickersgill) asked me to speak more slowly. It gives me pleasure to meet his request, but I must say I am surprised at his interest in the textile workers in view of the fact that as a member of the last Liberal federal cabinet he was among those largely responsible for sabotaging that Canadian industry.
Having read the statement of the workers union of Dominion Textile in Sherbrooke, I wish to say to the Minister of Labour (Mr. Starr) that I fully support the statement.
I urge him to intervene immediately, in order that an early solution be found to this difficult problem for the benefit of the workers concerned.
The second aspect of my remarks concerns the unemployment in the textile industry and the numerous difficulties this Canadian industry has to face on the domestic market.
Some 20 years ago, Canadian textile products were supplying about 75 per cent of the Canadian market. Today, they barely supply 44 per cent of the national market.
The major cause of this situation is the quantity of foreign textiles flooding the Canadian market.
In 1959, United States mills sold in Canada 6 million more yards of cotton than did Canadian mills.
Besides, in that same year, 1959, Japanese textile exports increased by 56 per cent.
The situation last year was becoming so alarming that the Minister of Finance (Mr. Fleming) made protests to the Japanese who agreed to voluntarily reduce their quotas in 1960. They were supposed to make their views known to us in December 1959, but they postponed their decision until early May, 1960.

Supply-Labour
However, in spite of our warnings, Japan continues to flood the Canadian market and its quotas, according to Mr. Laz Peters, chairman of the Canadian textile manufacturers association, are still excessive.
On the other hand, Mr. Chairman, a few months ago, when he visited Washington, the Minister of Finance asked the U.S. government to suspend subsidies on U.S. exports of textiles into Canada. The answer he got was a flat no.
Therefore, in the face of the U.S. government's categorical refusal, and of the Japanese government's tacit refusal, followed by a continued flooding of the Canadian market, it has become necessary for the Canadian government, after observing amenities at the international level, such as representations which I mentioned a moment ago, to fix its own quotas with regard to the most vulnerable sections of the textile industry.
It goes without saying that the present government, since it came into office has done a great deal in its attempt to settle this difficult problem of textile products that we have inherited from the previous Liberal administration.
Allow me to summarize under 12 headings the efforts made by this government in this field.
1.	In 1957, a request for inquiry and recommendations to the tariff board concerning tariff duties on textile products;
2.	In 1958, the present government raised the British preferential tariff on woollens and carded wool under tariff item 554b;
3.	In 1959, the government raised the most favoured nation tariff with regard to section 554b, in order to restore the British preferential margin;
4.	In 1958, the government considerably amended the definition of the word "dumping" in the act;
5.	Additional assessors were appointed and instructions were given to request more detailed invoices, accompanied by samples;
6.	Certain measures are taken with regard to particular groups of commodities on receipt of recommendations from the tariff board, while due consideration is given to the overall pattern of our international agreements;
7.	Negotiations were started in Geneva during the 1959 GATT session, with regard to a number of textile products;
